#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba1699a-1266-5d2a-83a1-9ef98174bfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412769} {:answer/id #uuid "1d712324-050d-5573-a4a5-5a0cca8602dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412770} {:answer/id #uuid "346b10b5-2bec-56ce-ae2c-3edb9c4195a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4xy=\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412772} {:answer/id #uuid "4da0e1b1-496c-56ae-a84c-c4de8d1b4756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412773} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d36e89-9f24-5514-94a7-4cfb223701bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4da0e1b1-496c-56ae-a84c-c4de8d1b4756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412773} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fa89b3-8d8c-5d7d-a143-81330cce5025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412775}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d7eb342-85c3-52e0-b57f-d62a1be729ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-103560" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{y-1}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\frac{1}{y-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e6e851-77d9-50d0-b99d-b8ca4b35fe00" :answer/type {:db/ident 