ac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82151a50-8f46-524b-8764-de2213dbd44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346489} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7594f9-73f0-5ae8-8637-57d291faea9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348019}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "876f8272-d67c-527c-9e50-16e055f2bb7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79750" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the right-hand side depends on $y.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "602a1896-949c-5ba4-acec-b55a0e8ffb0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346182} {:answer/id #uuid "70fc76d6-b92e-56b8-919f-75c301dc22b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346347} {:answer/id #uuid "7916f873-18e8-572e-8bfe-c7eb07bc42b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe95e83e-7f9c-5f03-be67-6f0472843ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347590} {:answer/id #uuid "feb69fd7-991c-5452-a24a-67e0a548634f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe95e83e-7f9c-5f03-be67-6f0472843ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347590} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a3e611-18f5-50b6-9382-4a1c3b83bb89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347648}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2bd0b60-25b3-5c98-bfdd-d8e9f9648972" :question/math-academy-id "q-79752" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\sqrt{y}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4f02d9-d19d-53f6-8dce-816c86d4c955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345763} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc06d89-f31e-5c76-8552-7a0c27b6fa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346577} {:answer/id #uuid "a6760f68-e23b-5712-9777-f8590af07b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\frac{1}{y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346804} {:answer/id #uuid "af27e45a-263e-5bda-a1f2-c7311c60bd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346878} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db124f-5fbb-5ee0-ba29-fedcd2002552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{(xy)}=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc06d89-f31e-5c76-8552-7a0c27b6fa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346577} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fcdbbd5-f55e-534a-b1ce-604a533dab7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347805}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70cd3eea-ad7c-56e9-b2e7-84eddd1e158d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79753" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $Q(x)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} {:answer/id #uuid "50784efb-ac52-5b2a-930e-224a3c46e1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346039} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7e5b5f-00cc-57b5-b9a0-eabad58dd3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346165} {:answer/id #uuid "a42c961b-9393-58eb-a38e-3ff82db15d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346775} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e532ee-210c-54c5-b6ba-20b24eb7144c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bd8f24-231f-5b7f-ad71-f5ad51631cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347926}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c92a3ce0-666c-5499-94f8-c31ec02af3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79754" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eee3b1f-7860-522c-8e25-2ab9122c7049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} {:answer/id #uuid "5eefff0b-47f1-50f6-aa20-45a25564434f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{e}^{2x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397783} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbcb751-5dd5-5c6c-8ef4-1f0333f1da29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{y}={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397784} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c9d281-4555-5a44-af3e-d9d7b6b8f65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397785} {:answer/id #uuid "54197164-398c-585a-83dd-5174235ce646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eee3b1f-7860-522c-8e25-2ab9122c7049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} :answer-field/id #uuid "440a36ad-3e7f-5834-9223-8af4fce0e717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "420cf089-e2ca-55d0-b4d5-3e91f54aadf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103567" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =y\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-x{e}^{2x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2b1967-4af5-520c-811d-5f2fb33cc469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302736} {:answer/id #uuid "7b84a815-9470-55cc-8164-a6fb81f55251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303223} {:answer/id #uuid "e562f968-7867-523a-a569-53df8d43088c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d4c2cd-7975-5587-badc-859966cb0b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303891} {:answer/id #uuid "ebebf99c-d5e5-53b4-97ef-d68b99aecdfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e562f968-7867-523a-a569-53df8d43088c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303880} :answer-field/id #uuid "3326193e-0a0c-59c1-ad04-260c0af220a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "591724df-60a1-5a9d-9f71-6942e86af15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331601" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=4$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecfc9ad-0591-5d56-b725-3982bf5bcba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302848} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c5df2b-0e09-5631-978d-a7638ab3eb56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303481} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d10eb5-5969-5f1c-9bba-1b1eb48631dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303605} {:answer/id #uuid "e7884954-9537-5ded-91a1-1196e28f05f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303895} {:answer/id #uuid "ec041024-752a-551d-bc1a-6d7c54f985c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecfc9ad-0591-5d56-b725-3982bf5bcba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302848} :answer-field/id #uuid "f764d5e3-0408-5d5f-b201-353145c06dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304739}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7723c02-822f-5a83-b3af-429d938426d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331744" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} {:answer/id #uuid "a4be644b-3c50-5bb5-b637-a1dc0590c6bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303470} {:answer/id #uuid "be5348b7-6521-531c-9590-b40f2257782f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303623} {:answer/id #uuid "bface693-37fe-568c-a4fa-08c4b3ba5757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303630} {:answer/id #uuid "e424eab9-c2e2-5989-8b94-f4410f99f5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3bff16-f60d-5153-a071-915cdea9c3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304634}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcddfdb-c38a-5f92-b718-28036cb5e0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302637} {:answer/id #uuid "24531df4-5849-5e8d-b717-57d07a8550b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302686} {:answer/id #uuid "5a15be87-c9e8-5225-a673-4b26b316be25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303004} {:answer/id #uuid "66137ed2-2b14-58d0-bd8a-ff3d608e1ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303074} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d947d0-6f8d-54b2-932e-06f1d5e06c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ed9d4c5-c8ba-5702-acc9-702d6e5cd248" :question/math-academy-id "q-331724" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7603a78a-aca0-51e5-9c68-1571861f143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303180} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9253d0-497c-5ed8-9904-752fb5b447a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303413} {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} {:answer/id #uuid "b8164d95-12bd-5b59-aa2d-33ca3cd95e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303598} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b02410-a499-5764-8c6c-bc4fea9102cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f51f02b-20a7-5356-a7bf-6883df31265e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304251}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c450ce-db9f-5997-8c48-e9942b8b8389" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7684d3-767a-52e5-bcc5-d780b7faa6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302753} {:answer/id #uuid "51c3b1e8-d753-5298-9a6f-e67e6cb118db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302962} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3595a2-a38b-57d0-aaa4-4e8fbbaa1590" :answer/typ